{"name":"Fluorescent Fire","hex":"#984427","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"fluorescent","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of or relating to fluorescence.","Exhibiting or produced by fluorescence."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A fluorescent light."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/fluorescent"},{"term":"fire","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A (usually self-sustaining) chemical reaction involving the bonding of oxygen with carbon or other fuel, with the production of heat and the presence of flame or smouldering.","An instance of this chemical reaction, especially when intentionally created and maintained in a specific location to a useful end (such as a campfire or a hearth fire)."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Amazing; excellent."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/fire"}],"entries":[{"title":"Fluorescence","blocks":[{"text":"Fluorescence is one of two kinds of photoluminescence, the emission of light by a substance that has absorbed light or other electromagnetic radiation. When exposed to ultraviolet radiation, many substances will glow (fluoresce) with colored visible light. The color of the light emitted depends on the chemical composition of the substance. Fluorescent materials generally cease to glow nearly immediately when the radiation source stops. This distinguishes them from the other type of light emission, phosphorescence. Phosphorescent materials continue to emit light for some time after the radiation stops.\nThis difference in duration is a result of quantum spin effects."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/Fluorescent_minerals_hg.jpg/330px-Fluorescent_minerals_hg.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fluorescence","wikidata":"Q191807"},{"title":"Fire","blocks":[{"text":"Fire is the rapid oxidation of a fuel in the exothermic chemical process of combustion, releasing heat, light, and various reaction products.\nFlames, the most visible portion of the fire, are produced in the combustion reaction when the fuel reaches its ignition point temperature. Flames from hydrocarbon fuels consist primarily of carbon dioxide, water vapor, oxygen, and nitrogen. If hot enough, the gases may become ionized to produce plasma. The color and intensity of the flame depend on the type of fuel and composition of the surrounding gases."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/36/Large_bonfire.jpg/330px-Large_bonfire.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fire","wikidata":"Q3196"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/15/Adaptive-Evolution-of-Eel-Fluorescent-Proteins-from-Fatty-Acid-Binding-Proteins-Produces-Bright-pone.0140972.g001.jpg/500px-Adaptive-Evolution-of-Eel-Fluorescent-Proteins-from-Fatty-Acid-Binding-Proteins-Produces-Bright-pone.0140972.g001.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/15/Adaptive-Evolution-of-Eel-Fluorescent-Proteins-from-Fatty-Acid-Binding-Proteins-Produces-Bright-pone.0140972.g001.jpg/500px-Adaptive-Evolution-of-Eel-Fluorescent-Proteins-from-Fatty-Acid-Binding-Proteins-Produces-Bright-pone.0140972.g001.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Fluorescent marine organisms","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAdaptive-Evolution-of-Eel-Fluorescent-Proteins-from-Fatty-Acid-Binding-Proteins-Produces-Bright-pone.0140972.g001.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4d/Black_light_theatre_Prague_HILT_13.jpg/500px-Black_light_theatre_Prague_HILT_13.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4d/Black_light_theatre_Pragu